speechtext:
Mr. THOMAS REID (New Westminster) moved;
Whereas, goods or services between nations or people can only be paid for either by other goods or services, or by some unit of value acceptable to those desiring such goods or services, and which unit of value has for ages been the precious metals, silver and gold;
And whereas, for centuries past, silver was the only precious metal used, both as currency and as the unit of value for the interchange of goods and services;
And whereas, latterly, gold was alone used by the leading nations of the world as the one unit of value for the exchange of goods and services and for the issuing of paper currency;
And whereas, the adoption of trade barriers and restrictions by many nations coupled with the very limited amount of gold available in the world for the interchange of goods and services has in large measure contributed to, if not brought about the unemployment of millions of people with its consequent untold misery and suffering;
And whereas, due to economic and existing monetary conditions many thousands of Canadian citizens have had to be sustained or given employment by the state;
And whereas, Canada has been borrowing large sums to provide for the relief of the unemployed and destitute;
And whereas, the issuing of currency in Canada has been based on certain coverage of gold, thereby restricting the amount of currency issued;
Therefore be it resolved,-That, in the opinion of this house, the government take immediate steps to ado-pt silver in addition to that of gold for the issuance of currency either in the form of paper certificates or as coinage, and that silver he again recognized and accepted as legal tender by the Dominion of Canada at a fixed ratio to that of gold.
He said; Mr. Speaker, in offering this resolution to the house I should like at the outset to say that I am doing so, not with the idea that its adoption will bring any panacea for the impasse, economic and monetary, existing throughout the world to-day, but rather that it would go a long way towards the stabilization of currency which I think will be generally agreed is more necessary to-day than at any other time in our history. To put into effect the proposal contained in the resolution would at least be a step in the right direction.
I realize, of course, that the worshippers of gold, those who advocate the single standard of gold, are in the majority and that those who have advocated and are advocating that
Bimetallic Currency-Mr. Reid

The market price. We
can discuss gold and silver as much as we like, but I submit that the main factor which we must keep before us is that portion of the resolution which provides that Canada shall fix immediately silver at a given ratio to gold.
For the reasons I have indicated, I submit that instead of having the beneficial effects Which my hon. friend from New Westminster (Mr. Reid) has suggested, it would have precisely the opposite effect, and would be perhaps one of the most damaging things we could possibly do to our currency.
My time is almost up, and as I have an amendment to submit I will present it at once. It is moved by myself and seconded by Mr. Stevens:
That all the words after the word "whereas" in the first line thereof be struck out, and the following substituted therefor:
"at the world monetary and economic conference held in London, England, in the year 1933, attended by the representatives of sixty-[Mr. Morand.l
four nations, the subject of the use of silver in the world's monetary systems was taken under consideration;
And whereas the following resoluton was unanimously passed

Be it resolved to recommend to all the governments parties to this conference V. (a) That an agreement be sought between the chief silver producing countries and those countries which are the largest holders or users of silver, with a view to mitigating fluctuations in the price of silver; and that the other nations not parties to such agreement should refrain from measures which could appreciably affect the silver market;
(b)	That governments parties to this conference shall refrain from new legislative measures which would involve further debasement of their silver coinage below a fineness of 800/1090;
(c)	That they shall substitute silver coins for low value paper currency in so far as the budgetary and local conditions of each country will permit;
_ (d') That all of the provisions of this resolution are subject to the following exceptions and limitations:
The requirements of such provisions shall lapse on April 1, 1934, if the agreement recommended in paragraph (a) does not come into force by that date, and in no case shall extend beyond January 1, 1938;
Governments may take any action relative to their silver coinage that they may deem necessary to prevent the flight or destruction of their silver coinage by reason of a rise in the bullion price of the silver content of their coin above the nominal or parity value of such silver coin;
And whereas on the 22nd day of July, 1933, Canada signed a memorandum of agreement regarding silver, together with Australia, China, United States of America, India, Mexico, Peru and Spain, which agreement has ben laid on the table of the house;
And whereas bimetallism is only practicable if adopted by substantially all the leading nations of the world;
And whereas the above resolution and agreement represented the greatest measure of international agreement which it was possible to obtain;
Therefore be it resolved that the action of the delegate of Canada in signing said agreement is hereby approved."
At six o'clock the house took recess.
After Recess
The house resumed at eight o'clock.
